  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =(x-1)^{3}{e}^{x}+3(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x}(x-1+3) \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}(x+2){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}(x+2){e}^{x}=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-2,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(0) & =(0-1)^{2}(0+2){e}^{0}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =(2-1)^{2}(2+2){e}^{2}=4{e}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $[0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2197$ |  | $\u2197$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $[0,\\infty ).$",
